body:
**Shape.** An owned local consumed on one path of an `if` is maybe-consumed after the join (K7.4) and must be disposed per path at scope end; r35 rejects the scope end instead.

```bezel
module Probe;

public sealed class Q
  private _id: int;
  public constructor(id: int)
    _id := id;
  end constructor;
  destructor
    Console.WriteLine($"D:{_id}");
  end destructor;
  public method Use()
    Console.WriteLine($"U:{_id}");
  end Use;
end Q;

public sealed class R
  private _id: int;
  public owned f: Q;
  public constructor(id: int, owned q: Q)
    _id := id;
    f := q;
  end constructor;
  destructor
    Console.WriteLine($"D:{_id}");
  end destructor;
  public method Use()
    Console.WriteLine($"U:{_id}");
  end Use;
  public static method Take(owned r: R)
    using var k := move r;
  end Take;
  public static method Body(flag: bool)
    var b := new R(2, new Q(1));
    if flag then
      R.Take(move b);                 // consumed on one path only: b is maybe-consumed after the join (K7.4)
    end;
  end Body;                           // K7.4: "scope-end disposal per-path"; r35 rejects the drop instead
end R;

public class Program
  public static method Main(args: Array<string>): int
    R.Body(args.Count > 0);
    Console.WriteLine("END");
    return 0;
  end Main;
end Program;
```

**Measured on r35.** `--checked-disposal` (and unchecked alike): compile error `owned value in \`b\` would be dropped here without disposal (§28.3 — consider \`using var\`, explicit \`.Dispose()\`, \`move\`, or \`return\`)` at the `end` of `Body`. The control with `b.Dispose();` in place of `R.Take(move b);` compiles and runs correctly on both paths (`D:2 D:1 END` with the disposal at the kill site on one path and at scope end on the other) — so the per-path release exists for the explicitly-disposed join and is missing for the moved-from one. `var a := move b;` and `using var a := move b;` in the arm behave like `Take(move b)`.

**Spec (v0.345).** K7.4 (§28.3.1): *"owned ⊓ moved-from and owned ⊓ explicitly-disposed = **maybe-consumed** — unreadable after the join, no flow around a back edge into a use; **scope-end disposal per-path**"*; K1 item 4: *"maybe-consumed — … scope-end disposal is per-path"*; K3.14: the implicit drop of a plain-`var` local *"carries the must-use warning (a lint over a sound program)"*. Expected: accept with the must-use warning, `D:2 D:1` at scope end only on the path that did not consume.

**Related.** #3967 / #5810 (open spec-tracking): the compiler's *reassignment* of a maybe-consumed local is a deliberately narrow rejection *"to lift once the per-path drop flag exists at reassignment"*; this is the same missing per-path flag at scope end, where no rejection is deliberate.

**Differential numbers.** 36 of 3,000 sampled programs in the pins/temps `--if` sample (vars 2 / len 4) are this shape — every model-accepted program whose `if` consumes a local by `move`/owned-argument on one arm and lets it reach scope end on the other.

**Measured on r35** (`bezelc 0.0.0-f6863e62`, the 2026-10-27 release build; main may have moved since). Found by the model-vs-compiler differential (`scratch/bzdiff`, proposal 0047 §5.3 / soundness README §3.2): the executable ownership model (bzcore L1, spec v0.345) and `bezelc` disagree on this program; the model's verdict is the spec's sentence quoted above.
